course: dictionary
lesson: 6491
title: Neap tides
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Tides are the rise and fall of the sea. The Moon's pull is the main cause of them. A neap tide is the tide with the smallest difference between high water and low water. «Neap tides, the smallest, come at half moon.» Here the smallest tide is the neap tide.
explain: Neap tides happen twice a month, around the quarter Moons. The Sun and Moon pull at right angles to each other, so their pulls do not add together much. Sailors and fishermen check the tides, so they know when the water will be low or high.
explain: Spring tides are the opposite. «Spring tides, the biggest, come at full moon and new moon, when the Sun and Moon pull in a line.» Neap and spring are the two kinds of tide you compare. Look for the word that tells you the size before you decide which is which.
fact: A tide is the rise and fall of the sea.
fact: The Moon's pull is the main cause of tides.
fact: A «neap tide» is a tide with the smallest difference between high and low water.
fact: Neap tides happen twice a month, around the quarter Moons.
fact: «Spring tides» are the biggest tides, at new and full Moon.
exercise: «Spring tides, the biggest, come at full moon and new moon.» What does neap mean? | A tide that comes in only at night | The biggest tide of the month | *The tide with the smallest difference between high and low water | A big wave made by a ship
exercise: According to the text, which tides are the smallest? | New moon tides | Full moon tides | Spring tides | *Neap tides
exercise: «Spring tides, the biggest, come at full moon and new moon.» Find and copy the word that means largest. | moon | tides | *biggest | full
test: When do neap tides happen, according to the lesson? | Once a year | *Around the quarter Moons | Only at full moon | Only at new moon
test: Which words describe a neap tide? | Very large waves | A tide with no water at all | *The smallest difference between high and low water | A tide that comes once a year
exam: A neap tide happens when the Sun and Moon pull at what angle to each other? | Upside down | *At right angles | Straight in a line | Very close together
